 16k's loose play in the middle gave the lead. After 13 F5 the analysis had White ahead by 10.0, and it never dropped below that for long. Opus 5.5 gave back a lot in the middle game, but the 16k kept paying the points back.", "moments": [{"turn": 13, "text": "The 16k's F5 was the biggest loss of the game: 8.3 points, taking Black's lead from -1.6 to -10.0. The engine wanted H6. Until then Black's E-file wall and White's G-file wall had been roughly even."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "White's C8 matched the engine's first choice and lost nothing (-10.7 → -10.8). It was a calm move in the top-left, where the position was already settled."}, {"turn": 43, "text": "The 16k's F1 lost 7.4 points, moving the lead from -2.8 to -10.2. It gave back nearly everything Black had won when Opus 5.5's C1 on move 42 lost 5.5. The engine preferred G1 there."}, {"turn": 52, "text": "Opus 5.5's H1 was its worst move, 7.3 points lost (-17.7 → -10.4), with A7 the engine's choice. It fit a stretch where both sides lost 6 to 7.5 points a move, from J3 on move 47 through F8 on move 53. Each side's slip answered the other's, and White stayed well ahead."}, {"turn": 58, "text": "B6 lost 6.0 points (-13.8 → -7.8), and the engine wanted B5. The 16k then took B5 itself, losing another 2.4. The left-edge fight ended with White still comfortably ahead, and White's later moves there gave up very little."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py and count.py only parse a pasted board and compute group liberties and area/territory counts for the current position, and the games.md commands only read or append game logs; none of them generates, scores or ranks candidate moves or looks ahead.", "stop": false}}